joining us once again on the program the  vaccines minister nadeem zahari mr zowie  always good to have you on and  as always a very busy day very busy week  for you and the rest of the government  clearly everyone is very keen to  discover how and  when we might be exiting lockdown and  and at the core of all of that are the  the four tests that the prime minister  is deploying run us through those  good morning everyone yes of course so  it will be  first of all the vaccine program  continuing uh at pace so we will  offer the vaccine to the over 50s by the  middle of april  and all of the adult population by the  end of  july the second is that the vaccines are  working and that we're seeing a  reduction in infection  hospitalization of course death uh the  third  of which is that infection rates  are not rising because of the different  uh parts of the opening up of the  economy to a level which would  overwhelm the nhs and of course in terms  of hospitalization  and the fourth is variance i.e that we  are  managing to keep uh variants under  control  and that we're not seeing what we saw  sadly  uh just before christmas which is the  new variant becoming more infectious and  the dominant variant  in the country and leading to the  large-scale hospitalization that we saw  in uh december january and of course  even now  so they are the four tests it is uh  making sure that  we proceed at each step and are able to  assess and then flex uh accordingly but  you'll hear more from the prime minister  both in the commons when he presents the  roadmap but also tonight in the press  conference  sure and there will be parents the  length and breadth of england  uh looking enviously at the date of  march the eighth  for all the schools to reopen however  just in terms of the four tests  that you've outlined the second of those  the evidence showing that vaccines are  reducing hospitalizations  and deaths in those vaccinated do we yet  have that evidence do we yet have that  information  certainly not in the public domain by my  reading so  the prime minister will say more about  this but you will know that public  health england have been running a  couple of large-scale studies  uh the siren study which tests the  frontline  health care staff who were in group two  if you recall of the vaccination program  so very early on  and uh another study called the valdi  which has been testing  care home residents again they were  group one and of course the other  evidence but uh the prime minister will  be saying more about that  uh suffice to say the evidence looks  good the oxford team  demonstrated their own evidence of uh  cutting transmission by  uh two thirds and of course um you know  we are  um we wouldn't be in this place this  morning to be able to say that we're  going to reopen schools on the  8th of march and of course as the school  holidays begin on the 29th of march  we will look at the rule of six and two  families being  to um uh see each other outdoors  if we haven't you know if we're not  confident that actually the vaccine  program is beginning to really  uh bear fruit sure can we just be  absolutely clear though with with all  schools reopening on  on march the 8th the expectation has to  be  that the number of infections will  increase  so that that as i say to you at the  beginning  all of that goes into the gradual  cautious reopening of schools and then  gradually reopening the rest of the  economy  as we vaccinate more people it is not a  coincidence  that the 8th of march is the date that  we have focused on because  if you take the mid-april date where we  will have  given at least one dose to all  over 50s offered that one dose  protection to all  over 50s if you go three weeks after the  middle of april that takes you to the  first week  of march now that phase one is 99  of mortality so it's no coincidence that  we are  uh opening schools after three weeks of  uh protecting  the over 50s and that phase one and then  we'll carry on  i get that sorry sorry to interrupt  minister the the reason is  we have had for for a few days now in  fact and from yourself and on the  program last week that the way out of  this is to be incredibly  cautious is to open things gradually i  mean the one aspect of the road map in  which  it doesn't feel particularly gradual is  getting kids back into schools you look  at what wales you look at scotland  they're going  in a phased return to school so they can  look at the data  see what happens in schools as as  children go back  why are we not doing that because it  doesn't feel cautious it feels somewhat  in congress actually  to reopen all the schools on one day and  say that this is a cautious approach  well i would say to you it's actually  quite the opposite if you look at  where hospitalization and of course  death  has uh come from with this pandemic it  is from  that one to nine group all the way from  the over 80s  the those obviously um that are  clinically extremely vulnerable but then  also  the uh over 50. so we're waiting  until all of that group the over 50s  have got the protection  i.e three weeks after getting the first  dose  before we reopen schools and of course  we do know that  uh infection around amongst children is  very very  and so this is a cautious  slow and deliberate reopening  uh as we continue of course to assess  the uh four tests that you and i have  just described to your viewers  i i i understand that that certainly  some of the evidence that  suggests that transmissibility amongst  children is it it's not as much of an  issue as it is amongst you know yourself  or myself but  you only need to look at for example i  mean there are examples of this all  around the world the situation in  british columbia and canada at a moment  where the  the kent variant is right running havoc  through the school system there a number  of schools having to have temporary  closures  look we understand that the plan as  outlined by the government is that we  don't roll back once  something is relaxed that is the way of  it but you have to say that  there could well be a big pause in this  roadmap  coming as we look at the data that  emerges from the school system  so so hence why the the fourth test if  you go back to your first question  um the dominant variant in the uk is the  one that you  refer to as the uh ken varian the  vaccines  very high efficacy against the dominant  variant which is why we're confident  on the vaccine effectiveness  the vaccine rollout continues to be uh  you know  going to plan we've in fact brought it  forward  more ambitious by mid april to offer the  vaccine to  over 50 year olds all over 50 year olds  but there is  a a you know a butt in there which is to  say  we have to make sure that infection  rates and hospitalization  also remain under control which is the  third test and the fourth test  back to your canadian example is that we  keep control  uh on variance and make sure that we  have that  ability to sequence which we have genome  sequence  largest one of the largest in the world  we account for about 50 of all  sequencing of covert  muted mutations and of course  surge testing and isolation as we've  been doing  uh with the variants that we've  discovered from south africa for example  and make sure we keep that under review  and control  so it is cautious we have to be careful  we want it to be sustainable as you  quite rightly described  and can we talk about the the second key  date in all of this march the 29th  which which you've already mentioned of  course the the suggestion is that might  be the point that we see the end to  stay at home indeed at the end to stay  local  plenty people up and down the country  myself included will be wondering  if that is the point at which those of  us who live  some distance from our families might be  able to go and see them  outside of course you're not allowed  indoors it is your reading of the the  relaxation that  people can travel at a distance to see  their family  uh on march the 29th if everything goes  well  quite right if everything goes well uh  this is outdoor  versus indoor we know there is a plenty  about to suggest that  outdoor the transmission is much lower  it is allowing two families to be able  to uh come together outdoors or the rule  of six  and it is a national um rule rather than  a regional or tiered uh rules so  um i think the important thing is to  wait to  hear from the prime minister this  afternoon in parliament but also  this evening on the details sure sure  but i i will push a little bit on this  and my producer working with me this  morning he lives in leeds it might be  possible for him  to get home and come back in a day  whereas  for me to go home and see my family  would potentially involve an  overnight stay in scotland of the two  are either of those in your mind either  not possible not permitted or not  advisable  so as long as it's outdoors and it is  two families or the rule of six  uh then that is what will be committed  if the  four tests continue uh to uh be  delivered  upon then that will be the national  um lockdown of course scotland uh as you  mentioned  and uh northern ireland and wales will  be setting out their own  um road map towards  reopening uh their uh economies  as well so at the moment uh the focus is  very much  on you know steady as she goes outdoor  versus indoor priority being uh children  in schools  second priority is obviously allowing  two people on the 8th of march to meet  outside for a coffee uh uh to sort of  address some of the issues  around loneliness and of course mental  health as well  and then the 29th is two families or  rule of six coming together and outdoor  sports  activities as well tennis can we then  sorry minister can we then just just be  absolutely candid  even on the roadmap as i understand it  even in the best case scenario  pubs restaurants non-essential shops  hairdressers  beauty treatments and the like none of  that will be happening even in the best  case scenario  until the other side of easter well i  however much tempting it may be neil i  will not preempt my prime minister  before he  stands up uh in the chamber the dispatch  box and  describes the road map to  parliamentarians into the country and of  course  at the press conference tonight i think  uh it's important that  uh yeah we we do this cautiously  steadily  based on the vaccination out which has  been you know i think  you know in many ways one of the great  achievements  of the coming together the nhs armed  forces the volunteers  the doctors nurses the private sector  that delivered  the vaccine both in terms of the  scientists and the manufacturers but  also they're delivering now for us in  the deployment  with the distribution so a lot of  hard work has gone in and we don't want  this hard-won  uh you know achievement  to be in some way jeopardized by uh  us you know emotionally feeling that  we've got to try and go faster we've got  to be  led later not the date very very quickly  because i know you have to go  can you offer a cast iron set in stone  guarantee that there will never be  another national lockdown  i think the important thing is to make  it sustainable you're absolutely right  without the prime minister if i look at  my own portfolio  has tasked us not only to deliver the  the largest vaccination program in the  history  of the nhs of this country but also to  look at  variant vaccines that may be needed  as an autumn booster if we need it  and of course a longer term uh future  proofing of the vaccination program so  i'm confident that if we do this  cautiously  and we do it based on the data the  evidence then it will be sustainable  and it should be the last time we ever  enter the severe  level of lockdown because of covert 19.

